\chapter{Asynchronous Programming\label{CHPTASYNC}}

\index{asynchronous programming}%
\index{fibers}%
\index{tasks}%
This chapter describes {\ChezScheme}'s asynchronous programming subsystem.
The asynchronous libraries provide lightweight cooperative tasks
(fibers), structured task groups, hierarchical cancellation contexts,
composable wait operations, bounded channels, fiber-aware mutexes, and high-performance
nonblocking input/output (I/O) facilities.

Tasks are VM-native fibers scheduled within a \emph{scheduler group}.
A scheduler group manages one or more schedulers, each executing on an
underlying operating-system thread with an integrated event loop powered by
libuv.  Tasks marked as migratable may be automatically transferred among
schedulers in the same group via work stealing, enabling scalable
multicore concurrency without requiring low-level thread synchronization by
the programmer.
An I/O wait resumes first on the scheduler that owns its registration.  The
task becomes eligible for work stealing again after that resumed turn.

The asynchronous scheduler is entered by invoking \scheme{run-async}.
Procedures described as blocking in this chapter suspend only the calling
asynchronous task, allowing other tasks on the same scheduler to continue
execution.  Such procedures must be invoked from within the dynamic extent
of a call to \scheme{run-async}.

Waitable actions are represented by first-class \emph{operations}, whose
constructors have names ending in \scheme{-operation}.  An operation describes
a wait condition without immediately performing it.  Operations can be
transformed, dynamically selected, and combined using combinators such as
\scheme{choice-operation} and \scheme{wrap-operation} in the style of
Concurrent ML (CML).

The core asynchronous subsystem is organized into six primary libraries:

\schemedisplay
(chezscheme async)
(chezscheme async context)
(chezscheme async operations)
(chezscheme async channels)
(chezscheme async sync)
(chezscheme async syntax)
\endschemedisplay

In addition, modular I/O domain libraries provide nonblocking networking,
filesystem operations, subprocess management, signal handling, event
watching, and system diagnostics:

\schemedisplay
(chezscheme async io errors)
(chezscheme async io stream)
(chezscheme async io dns)
(chezscheme async io udp)
(chezscheme async io fs)
(chezscheme async io poll)
(chezscheme async io process)
(chezscheme async io signal)
(chezscheme async io watch)
(chezscheme async io tty)
(chezscheme async io system)
\endschemedisplay

The underlying libuv implementation is statically linked into the
{\ChezScheme} runtime kernel and does not require external shared
libraries.  Port adapters are provided to allow asynchronous streams and files
to be used seamlessly with standard {\ChezScheme} binary port procedures.


\section{Schedulers and Tasks\label{SECTASYNCTASKS}}

\index{schedulers}%
\index{tasks}%
An asynchronous scheduler manages the cooperative execution of tasks and
dispatches I/O events.  The entry point to the scheduler is
\scheme{run-async}.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{run-async}{\categoryprocedure}{(run-async \var{thunk} \var{option} \var{value} \dots)}
\returns the values returned by \var{thunk}
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\var{thunk} must be a procedure that accepts zero arguments.
\scheme{run-async} establishes an asynchronous scheduler group, creates a
root task that executes \var{thunk}, and runs the scheduler until the root
task's structured task group terminates.

Optional configuration settings may be supplied as alternating
\var{option} and \var{value} pairs.  The supported options are:

\begin{description}
\item[\scheme{clock}] specifies the scheduler clock and must be either the
  symbol \scheme{real} (the default) or \scheme{virtual}.  A virtual clock
  advances immediately to the next scheduled timer whenever no tasks are
  ready to run, which is useful for deterministic simulation and testing.
\item[\scheme{parallelism}] must be a positive fixnum specifying the number
  of worker operating-system threads in the scheduler group (defaults to 1).
  A value greater than 1 requires a threaded build of {\ChezScheme} and a
  \scheme{real} clock.
\item[\scheme{preemption-ticks}] must be either \scheme{#f} (the default) or
  a fixnum of at least 1000.  When specified as a tick budget, time-sliced
  preemptive scheduling is enabled for Scheme computations.  Timer
  expiration transfers the native task fiber to its scheduler at a Scheme
  safe point;
  a migratable task remains eligible for work stealing when it becomes ready.
  Preemption is deferred while native-fiber switching is prohibited or a VM
  transition is active.  \scheme{run-async} cannot execute inside an active
  engine, an engine cannot be invoked from an async task, and an async task
  cannot invoke a nested \scheme{run-async}.
\end{description}

\scheme{run-async} returns the values returned by \var{thunk}.  If the root
task terminates due to an unhandled exception or cancellation condition,
that condition is re-raised by \scheme{run-async}.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{spawn-task}{\categoryprocedure}{(spawn-task \var{thunk} \var{option} \var{value} \dots)}
\returns a task object
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\var{thunk} must be a procedure that accepts zero arguments.
\scheme{spawn-task} creates and schedules a new concurrent task that invokes
\var{thunk}.

Optional settings may be supplied as alternating \var{option} and
\var{value} pairs:

\begin{description}
\item[\scheme{name}] specifies an application-defined identifier for the
  task (typically a symbol or string) or \scheme{#f} (the default).
\item[\scheme{group}] specifies the explicit task group to which the new
  task belongs.  If omitted, the task belongs to the ambient task group.
\item[\scheme{context}] specifies an explicit parent cancellation context
  for the task.  If omitted, the task inherits a child of its task group's
  context or the ambient dynamic context.
\item[\scheme{migratable?}] must be a boolean specifying whether the task
  is eligible for work stealing across worker threads in a multi-threaded
  scheduler group (defaults to \scheme{#f}).
\end{description}

Each task automatically receives and owns a fresh child cancellation
context.  When the task terminates, its owned context is automatically
canceled so that descendant operations do not outlive the task.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{task?}{\categoryprocedure}{(task? \var{obj})}
\formdef{task-id}{\categoryprocedure}{(task-id \var{task})}
\formdef{task-name}{\categoryprocedure}{(task-name \var{task})}
\formdef{task-state}{\categoryprocedure}{(task-state \var{task})}
\formdef{task-scheduler}{\categoryprocedure}{(task-scheduler \var{task})}
\formdef{task-current-wait}{\categoryprocedure}{(task-current-wait \var{task})}
\formdef{task-context}{\categoryprocedure}{(task-context \var{task})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{task?} returns \scheme{#t} if \var{obj} is a task object and
\scheme{#f} otherwise.
\scheme{task-id} returns a unique exact integer assigned to \var{task}.
\scheme{task-name} returns the name supplied when \var{task} was spawned,
or \scheme{#f} if none was provided.
\scheme{task-state} returns a symbol representing the current lifecycle state
of \var{task}, which is one of \scheme{created}, \scheme{ready},
\scheme{running}, \scheme{waiting}, \scheme{completed}, \scheme{failed}, or
\scheme{canceled}.
\scheme{task-scheduler} returns the scheduler instance that currently hosts
\var{task}.
\scheme{task-current-wait} returns diagnostic data describing the operation
on which \var{task} is currently suspended, or \scheme{#f} if the task is
not waiting.
\scheme{task-context} returns the cancellation context owned by \var{task}.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{current-async-task}{\categoryprocedure}{(current-async-task)}
\formdef{current-async-scheduler}{\categoryprocedure}{(current-async-scheduler)}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{current-async-task} returns the task currently executing on the
calling thread, or \scheme{#f} if called outside an asynchronous task.
\scheme{current-async-scheduler} returns the scheduler instance hosting the
calling thread, or \scheme{#f} if called outside an asynchronous scheduler.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{task-join}{\categoryprocedure}{(task-join \var{task})}
\returns the values returned by \var{task}
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\var{task} must be a task object.
\scheme{task-join} suspends the calling task until \var{task} terminates.
If \var{task} completes successfully, \scheme{task-join} returns all values
produced by \var{task}.  If \var{task} terminated with an uncaught exception
or cancellation condition, that condition is re-raised in the calling task.
An exception is raised if a task attempts to join itself.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{task-join-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(task-join-operation \var{task})}
\returns an operation
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\var{task} must be a task object.
\scheme{task-join-operation} constructs the operation performed by
\scheme{task-join}.  If the operation loses a choice, its waiter is withdrawn
without canceling \var{task}.  An exception is raised if the operation targets
the task that performs it.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{task-cancel!}{\categoryprocedure}{(task-cancel! \var{task})}
\formdef{task-cancel!}{\categoryprocedure}{(task-cancel! \var{task} \var{reason})}
\returns unspecified
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\var{task} must be a task object.
\scheme{task-cancel!} cancels the cancellation context owned by \var{task}.
If \var{reason} is supplied, it becomes the cancellation reason; otherwise,
the default reason is used.  Cancellation is idempotent: subsequent calls to
\scheme{task-cancel!} have no effect, and the first cancellation reason is
preserved and propagated to all descendant contexts.  Cancellation takes
effect at the next asynchronous cancellation checkpoint.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{task-yield}{\categoryprocedure}{(task-yield)}
\returns a boolean
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{task-yield} cooperatively yields execution of the current task,
placing it at the end of the scheduler's ready queue and allowing other
runnable tasks to execute.  It returns \scheme{#t} when called from within an
asynchronous task, and \scheme{#f} when called outside an asynchronous task.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-sleep}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-sleep \var{seconds})}
\returns unspecified
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\var{seconds} must be a nonnegative real number.
\scheme{async-sleep} suspends the calling task for at least \var{seconds}
without blocking the underlying operating-system thread.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-dynamic-wind}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-dynamic-wind \var{before} \var{thunk} \var{after})}
\returns the values returned by \var{thunk}
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\var{before}, \var{thunk}, and \var{after} must be procedures that accept zero
arguments.
\scheme{async-dynamic-wind} establishes dynamic-extent cleanup guards for
asynchronous tasks.  Native task switches preserve the dynamic extent, so the
\var{before} and \var{after} guards run upon normal entry and exit, non-local
jumps via continuations, uncaught exception unwinding, and cancellation
cleanup, but not during suspension, yielding, migration, or preemption.

\section{Task Groups\label{SECTASYNCTASKGROUPS}}

\index{task groups}%
\index{structured concurrency}%
Task groups provide structured concurrency by establishing lifetime boundaries
for sets of related tasks.  A task group ensures that child tasks do not
outlive their parent scope and aggregates unobserved child failures.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{make-task-group}{\categoryprocedure}{(make-task-group)}
\returns a new task group
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{make-task-group} creates a fresh task group that captures the ambient
cancellation context.  Tasks spawned into the group inherit a child of that
captured context unless an explicit \scheme{context} option is given.  Its
first spawned task associates the group with the enclosing structured
lifetime.  The group may be reused within that lifetime after
\scheme{task-group-wait} returns.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{task-group?}{\categoryprocedure}{(task-group? \var{obj})}
\returns \scheme{#t} if \var{obj} is a task group, \scheme{#f} otherwise
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{task-group?} is the type predicate for task groups.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{task-group-wait}{\categoryprocedure}{(task-group-wait \var{task-group})}
\returns unspecified
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\var{task-group} must be a task group object.
\scheme{task-group-wait} suspends the calling task until all child tasks and
active descendant groups belonging to \var{task-group} have terminated.  If
any child task terminated with an unhandled exception or failure,
\scheme{task-group-wait} re-raises the failure condition.


\section{Cancellation Conditions\label{SECTASYNCCANCELLATIONCONDITIONS}}

\index{cancellation conditions}%
\index{conditions!cancellation}%
Cancellation conditions are raised when an asynchronous task, context, or
operation is aborted due to explicit cancellation, a timeout, or a deadline.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{make-async-cancellation-condition}{\categoryprocedure}{(make-async-cancellation-condition)}
\formdef{make-async-cancellation-condition}{\categoryprocedure}{(make-async-cancellation-condition \var{reason})}
\formdef{async-cancellation-condition?}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-cancellation-condition? \var{obj})}
\formdef{async-cancellation-reason}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-cancellation-reason \var{condition})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{make-async-cancellation-condition} constructs a new cancellation
condition carrying the specified \var{reason}, or \scheme{#f} if omitted.
\scheme{async-cancellation-condition?} returns \scheme{#t} if \var{obj} is an
asynchronous cancellation condition and \scheme{#f} otherwise.
\scheme{async-cancellation-reason} returns the reason object stored within
\var{condition}.


\section{Scheduler Information and Metrics\label{SECTASYNCSCHEDULERINFO}}

\index{scheduler metrics}%
\index{diagnostics}%
The following procedures inspect scheduler state and execution metrics.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-scheduler?}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-scheduler? \var{obj})}
\formdef{async-scheduler-task-count}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-scheduler-task-count \var{scheduler})}
\formdef{async-scheduler-turn-count}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-scheduler-turn-count \var{scheduler})}
\formdef{async-scheduler-suspension-count}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-scheduler-suspension-count \var{scheduler})}
\formdef{async-scheduler-wakeup-count}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-scheduler-wakeup-count \var{scheduler})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

The predicate \scheme{async-scheduler?} recognizes scheduler objects.
The remaining procedures return, respectively, the total active task count
for the scheduler group, the number of event-loop turns for \var{scheduler},
the number of task suspensions, and the number of task resumptions.

The following example demonstrates spawning a migratable worker task across a
two-thread scheduler group, joining the task, and receiving its return values:

\schemedisplay
(import (chezscheme) (chezscheme async))

(run-async
  (lambda ()
    (let ([worker
           (spawn-task
             (lambda () (values 'answer 42))
             'name 'worker
             'migratable? #t)])
      (let-values ([(label value) (task-join worker)])
        (list label value))))
  'parallelism 2) ;=> (answer 42)
\endschemedisplay


\section{Cancellation Contexts\label{SECTASYNCCONTEXTS}}

\index{cancellation contexts}%
\index{timeouts}%
\index{deadlines}%
The \scheme{(chezscheme async context)} library provides hierarchical
cancellation contexts with support for timeouts, deadlines, and propagation.
Cancellation flows downward from parent contexts to all descendants, but does
not affect parent or sibling contexts.  All context inspection and
cancellation procedures are thread-safe.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{make-async-context}{\categoryprocedure}{(make-async-context)}
\formdef{make-async-context}{\categoryprocedure}{(make-async-context \var{parent})}
\returns an asynchronous context object
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{make-async-context} creates a fresh asynchronous context.  If
\var{parent} is omitted, the ambient asynchronous context is used as the
parent when one exists.  Passing \scheme{#f} for \var{parent} creates an
independent root context.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-context?}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-context? \var{obj})}
\formdef{async-context-canceled?}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-context-canceled? \var{context})}
\formdef{async-context-reason}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-context-reason \var{context})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{async-context?} returns \scheme{#t} if \var{obj} is an
asynchronous context, and \scheme{#f} otherwise.

\scheme{async-context-canceled?} returns \scheme{#t} if \var{context} has
been canceled, and \scheme{#f} otherwise.

\scheme{async-context-reason} returns the cancellation reason of
\var{context}, or \scheme{#f} if it has not been canceled.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-context-cancel!}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-context-cancel! \var{context})}
\formdef{async-context-cancel!}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-context-cancel! \var{context} \var{reason})}
\returns unspecified
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\var{context} must be an asynchronous context.
\scheme{async-context-cancel!} cancels \var{context} and all of its
descendants.  Cancellation is idempotent; the first supplied \var{reason} is
retained and propagated throughout the tree.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-context-done-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-context-done-operation \var{context})}
\returns an operation
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\var{context} must be an asynchronous context.
\scheme{async-context-done-operation} returns an operation that completes
with zero values as soon as \var{context} is canceled.  It can be combined
with other operations using \scheme{choice-operation} to interrupt waits.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{perform-operation/context}{\categoryprocedure}{(perform-operation/context \var{context} \var{operation})}
\returns the values produced by \var{operation}
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\var{context} must be an asynchronous context, and \var{operation} must be an
operation.
This procedure performs \var{operation} while racing it against the
cancellation of \var{context}.  If \var{context} is canceled before the
operation completes, the operation is withdrawn (nacked) and an asynchronous
cancellation condition carrying the context's cancellation reason is raised.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{current-async-context}{\categoryprocedure}{(current-async-context)}
\returns the ambient context or \scheme{#f}
\formdef{call-with-async-context}{\categoryprocedure}{(call-with-async-context \var{context} \var{thunk})}
\returns the values returned by \var{thunk}
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{current-async-context} returns the current task's ambient cancellation
context, or \scheme{#f} if none is active.
\scheme{call-with-async-context} installs \var{context} as the ambient
cancellation context for the dynamic extent of \var{thunk}.  Tasks spawned
within that extent inherit child contexts of \var{context}, and operations
performed by the current task are bounded by \var{context}.
The context carries cancellation and deadline ownership.  Application state
is passed explicitly through lexical bindings, closures, records, channels,
and operation values.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-context-with-timeout}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-context-with-timeout \var{parent} \var{seconds})}
\formdef{async-context-with-deadline}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-context-with-deadline \var{parent} \var{monotonic-time})}
\returns an asynchronous context
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

These procedures create child contexts that are automatically canceled with
the reason \scheme{deadline-exceeded} upon expiration.
\scheme{async-context-with-timeout} accepts a relative duration \var{seconds}
as a nonnegative real number and operates under both real and virtual
scheduler clocks.
\scheme{async-context-with-deadline} accepts an absolute \scheme{time-monotonic}
time object and requires a real scheduler clock.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{call-with-async-timeout}{\categoryprocedure}{(call-with-async-timeout \var{seconds} \var{thunk})}
\returns the values returned by \var{thunk}
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\var{seconds} must be a nonnegative real number, and \var{thunk} must be a
procedure that accepts zero arguments.
\scheme{call-with-async-timeout} creates a child context with the specified
relative timeout, installs it as the ambient context for the dynamic extent of
\var{thunk}, and cancels the timer upon normal or exceptional exit.

\schemedisplay
(import (chezscheme)
        (chezscheme async)
        (chezscheme async context))

(run-async
  (lambda ()
    (guard (c [(async-cancellation-condition? c)
               (async-cancellation-reason c)])
      (call-with-async-timeout 0.1
        (lambda ()
          (async-sleep 10)
          'completed))))) ;=> deadline-exceeded
\endschemedisplay


\section{Composable Operations\label{SECTASYNCOPERATIONS}}

\index{operations}%
\index{Concurrent ML}%
\index{futures}%
The \scheme{(chezscheme async operations)} library provides first-class,
composable waitable operations based on the Concurrent ML model.  An
operation can be performed immediately, wrapped to transform its result, or
combined with other operations in a non-deterministic choice.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{operation?}{\categoryprocedure}{(operation? \var{obj})}
\returns \scheme{#t} if \var{obj} is an operation, \scheme{#f} otherwise
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{operation?} is the type predicate for operations.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{perform-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(perform-operation \var{operation})}
\returns the values produced by \var{operation}
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\var{operation} must be an operation.
\scheme{perform-operation} executes \var{operation}.  If the operation cannot
complete immediately, the calling task is suspended until the operation is
ready or the task's cancellation context is triggered.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{make-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(make-operation \var{try} \var{block})}
\formdef{make-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(make-operation \var{try} \var{block} \var{wrap})}
\formdef{make-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(make-operation \var{try} \var{block} \var{wrap} \var{nack})}
\returns an operation
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{make-operation} is the low-level constructor for custom operations.
\var{try} is invoked with a synchronization state and must return
\scheme{#f} if the operation must suspend, a pair \scheme{(values . \var{list})}
to succeed immediately with \var{list} as return values, or a pair
\scheme{(raise . \var{condition})} to immediately raise an exception.
\var{block} is invoked when the operation must suspend, receiving the
synchronization state and a one-shot delivery procedure.  The optional
\var{wrap} procedure transforms successful return values.  The optional
\var{nack} procedure is invoked if another arm of a \scheme{choice-operation}
succeeds, withdrawing any registered waiter.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{always-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(always-operation \var{value} \dots)}
\formdef{never-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(never-operation)}
\formdef{sleep-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(sleep-operation \var{seconds})}
\returns an operation
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{always-operation} returns an operation that completes immediately,
producing \var{value} \dots.
\scheme{never-operation} returns an operation that never completes.
\scheme{sleep-operation} returns an operation that completes with zero values
after \var{seconds} have elapsed.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{wrap-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(wrap-operation \var{operation} \var{procedure})}
\returns an operation
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\var{operation} must be an operation, and \var{procedure} must be a procedure.
\scheme{wrap-operation} returns an operation that, upon successful completion
of \var{operation}, applies \var{procedure} to the produced values and returns
the results of that call.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{choice-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(choice-operation \var{operation} \dots)}
\returns an operation
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{choice-operation} combines multiple operations into a single choice.
When performed, exactly one ready child operation commits, while the others
are aborted (nacked).  To ensure fairness, the order in which child operations
are attempted alternates between successive executions.  If no child operations
are provided, \scheme{choice-operation} behaves like \scheme{never-operation}.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{make-future}{\categoryprocedure}{(make-future)}
\formdef{future?}{\categoryprocedure}{(future? \var{obj})}
\formdef{future-fulfil!}{\categoryprocedure}{(future-fulfil! \var{future} \var{value} \dots)}
\formdef{future-fail!}{\categoryprocedure}{(future-fail! \var{future} \var{condition})}
\formdef{future-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(future-operation \var{future})}
\formdef{future-get}{\categoryprocedure}{(future-get \var{future})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

A \emph{future} is a single-assignment asynchronous value container.
\scheme{make-future} creates an unfulfilled future.
\scheme{future?} returns \scheme{#t} if \var{obj} is a future and \scheme{#f} otherwise.
\scheme{future-fulfil!} resolves \var{future} with \var{value} \dots.
\scheme{future-fail!} rejects \var{future} with \var{condition}.
Attempting to fulfill or fail a future more than once raises an exception.
\scheme{future-operation} returns an operation that produces the future's
values or re-raises its failure condition.
\scheme{future-get} performs \scheme{future-operation} directly, suspending
the calling task until the future is resolved.

The following example uses \scheme{choice-operation} and \scheme{wrap-operation}
to race a channel read against a timer:

\schemedisplay
(import (chezscheme)
        (chezscheme async)
        (chezscheme async operations)
        (chezscheme async channels))

(run-async
  (lambda ()
    (let ([input (make-channel)])
      (spawn-task
        (lambda ()
          (async-sleep 0.01)
          (channel-put input "ready")))
      (perform-operation
        (choice-operation
          (channel-get-operation input)
          (wrap-operation (sleep-operation 1)
            (lambda () "timed out"))))))) ;=> "ready"
\endschemedisplay


\section{Channels\label{SECTASYNCCHANNELS}}

\index{channels}%
\index{message passing}%
The \scheme{(chezscheme async channels)} library implements typed, thread-safe
communication channels.  Channels support unbuffered rendezvous
synchronization as well as bounded FIFO buffering, and can be safely shared
across tasks, schedulers, and operating-system threads.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{make-channel}{\categoryprocedure}{(make-channel)}
\formdef{make-channel}{\categoryprocedure}{(make-channel \var{capacity})}
\returns a new channel
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{make-channel} creates a new channel.  The optional \var{capacity}
must be an exact nonnegative integer (defaulting to 0).  A capacity of 0
creates an unbuffered \emph{rendezvous} channel where senders and receivers
synchronize directly.  A positive capacity creates a bounded FIFO queue.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{channel?}{\categoryprocedure}{(channel? \var{obj})}
\returns \scheme{#t} if \var{obj} is a channel, \scheme{#f} otherwise
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{channel?} is the type predicate for channels.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{channel-close!}{\categoryprocedure}{(channel-close! \var{channel})}
\formdef{channel-close!}{\categoryprocedure}{(channel-close! \var{channel} \var{reason})}
\formdef{channel-closed?}{\categoryprocedure}{(channel-closed? \var{channel})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\var{channel} must be a channel.
\scheme{channel-close!} closes \var{channel}.  Closing is idempotent, and the
first supplied \var{reason} is retained.  Values already buffered in
\var{channel} remain readable in FIFO order until drained.  Subsequent or
blocked put operations fail immediately with a channel-closed condition.
\scheme{channel-closed?} returns \scheme{#t} if \var{channel} has been closed
and \scheme{#f} otherwise.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{make-channel-closed-condition}{\categoryprocedure}{(make-channel-closed-condition)}
\formdef{make-channel-closed-condition}{\categoryprocedure}{(make-channel-closed-condition \var{reason})}
\formdef{channel-closed-condition?}{\categoryprocedure}{(channel-closed-condition? \var{obj})}
\formdef{channel-closed-reason}{\categoryprocedure}{(channel-closed-reason \var{condition})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

These procedures construct, recognize, and inspect the condition raised when
attempting to write to a closed channel, or when reading from an empty closed
channel via \scheme{channel-get}.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{channel-put-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(channel-put-operation \var{channel} \var{value})}
\formdef{channel-get-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(channel-get-operation \var{channel})}
\formdef{channel-receive-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(channel-receive-operation \var{channel})}
\returns an operation
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{channel-put-operation} returns an operation that sends \var{value}
over \var{channel}, completing once the value is placed in the buffer or
accepted by a receiver.
\scheme{channel-get-operation} returns an operation that produces the next
value from \var{channel}, or raises a channel-closed condition if the channel
is closed and drained.
\scheme{channel-receive-operation} returns an operation that produces two
values: \scheme{\var{value} #t} when an item is received, or \scheme{#f #f}
when the channel is closed and drained.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{channel-put}{\categoryprocedure}{(channel-put \var{channel} \var{value})}
\formdef{channel-get}{\categoryprocedure}{(channel-get \var{channel})}
\formdef{channel-receive}{\categoryprocedure}{(channel-receive \var{channel})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

These procedures directly perform their corresponding channel operations.
\scheme{channel-put} transmits \var{value} and returns an unspecified value.
\scheme{channel-get} returns the next value, raising a channel-closed condition
if drained.
\scheme{channel-receive} returns two values: \var{value} and a boolean
\var{open?}, distinguishing a transmitted \scheme{#f} value from a drained
channel.

\schemedisplay
(import (chezscheme)
        (chezscheme async)
        (chezscheme async channels))

(run-async
  (lambda ()
    (let ([values (make-channel 2)])
      (spawn-task
        (lambda ()
          (channel-put values 1)
          (channel-put values 2)
          (channel-close! values)))
      (let loop ([items '()])
        (let-values ([(value open?) (channel-receive values)])
          (if open?
              (loop (cons value items))
              (reverse items))))))) ;=> (1 2)
\endschemedisplay


\section{Fiber-Aware Synchronization\label{SECTASYNCMUTEXES}}

\index{mutexes!asynchronous}%
\index{synchronization!fibers}%
The \scheme{(chezscheme async sync)} library provides task-suspending
synchronization primitives.  Waiting for an async mutex suspends the calling
task instead of blocking its scheduler thread.  Ownership belongs to the task
and is retained when the task yields, waits, is preempted, or migrates to
another scheduler in the same group.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{make-async-mutex}{\categoryprocedure}{(make-async-mutex)}
\returns a new async mutex
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

The new mutex is initially unowned.  Async mutexes are nonrecursive.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-mutex?}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-mutex? \var{obj})}
\returns \scheme{#t} if \var{obj} is an async mutex, \scheme{#f} otherwise
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-mutex-acquire-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-mutex-acquire-operation \var{mutex})}
\formdef{async-mutex-acquire}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-mutex-acquire \var{mutex})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{async-mutex-acquire-operation} constructs an operation that acquires
\var{mutex}.  It composes with choice, timeout, and context operations; a
canceled or losing acquisition is removed from the wait queue.
\scheme{async-mutex-acquire} performs that operation and returns an
unspecified value after ownership is transferred to the current task.
Contended waiters acquire ownership in FIFO registration order.  Attempting
to acquire a mutex already owned by the current task is an error.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-mutex-release!}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-mutex-release! \var{mutex})}
\returns unspecified
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

The current task must own \var{mutex}.  Ownership is transferred directly to
the first live waiter before that task is resumed.  Releasing an unowned mutex
or one owned by another task is an error.
When a task terminates, any mutexes still owned by that task are released
before its terminal state is published to joiners.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{call-with-async-mutex}{\categoryprocedure}{(call-with-async-mutex \var{mutex} \var{thunk})}
\returns the values returned by \var{thunk}
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{call-with-async-mutex} acquires \var{mutex}, invokes \var{thunk}, and
releases the mutex after a normal return or an exception, including task
cancellation.  Suspension and timed preemption during \var{thunk} retain
ownership.

\schemedisplay
(import (chezscheme)
        (chezscheme async)
        (chezscheme async sync))

(run-async
  (lambda ()
    (let ([mutex (make-async-mutex)] [counter 0])
      (let ([workers
             (map
               (lambda (id)
                 (spawn-task
                   (lambda ()
                     (do ([i 0 (+ i 1)]) ((= i 100))
                       (call-with-async-mutex mutex
                         (lambda () (set! counter (+ counter 1))))))
                   'migratable? #t))
               '(0 1 2 3))])
        (for-each task-join workers)
        counter)))
  'parallelism 4) ;=> 400
\endschemedisplay

\bigskip\noindent\textbf{Read/write mutexes.}\par

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{make-async-rw-mutex}{\categoryprocedure}{(make-async-rw-mutex)}
\returns a new async read/write mutex
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

The new mutex is initially unowned.  Read mode admits multiple tasks, while
write mode grants exclusive ownership.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-rw-mutex?}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-rw-mutex? \var{obj})}
\returns \scheme{#t} if \var{obj} is an async read/write mutex, \scheme{#f} otherwise
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-rw-mutex-acquire-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-rw-mutex-acquire-operation \var{mutex})}
\formdef{async-rw-mutex-read-acquire-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-rw-mutex-read-acquire-operation \var{mutex})}
\formdef{async-rw-mutex-acquire}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-rw-mutex-acquire \var{mutex})}
\formdef{async-rw-mutex-read-acquire}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-rw-mutex-read-acquire \var{mutex})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

The forms without \scheme{read} acquire write mode.  Once a writer is waiting,
subsequent first-time readers queue behind it.  A task may recursively acquire
read mode, with one release required for each acquisition.  Write mode is
nonrecursive.  Acquiring write mode while holding read mode, or acquiring read
mode while holding write mode, is an error.  The operation forms compose with
choice, timeout, and context operations.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-rw-mutex-release!}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-rw-mutex-release! \var{mutex})}
\formdef{async-rw-mutex-read-release!}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-rw-mutex-read-release! \var{mutex})}
\returns unspecified
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

The current task must own the corresponding write or read mode.  Task
termination releases any modes still owned by the terminating task.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{call-with-async-rw-mutex}{\categoryprocedure}{(call-with-async-rw-mutex \var{mutex} \var{thunk})}
\formdef{call-with-async-read-mutex}{\categoryprocedure}{(call-with-async-read-mutex \var{mutex} \var{thunk})}
\returns the values returned by \var{thunk}
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

The first procedure uses write mode and the second uses read mode.  Each
releases its mode after normal return, exception, or cancellation.

\bigskip\noindent\textbf{Wait groups.}\par

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{make-async-wait-group}{\categoryprocedure}{(make-async-wait-group)}
\formdef{make-async-wait-group}{\categoryprocedure}{(make-async-wait-group \var{count})}
\returns a new async wait group
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\var{count} defaults to zero and must be a nonnegative exact integer.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-wait-group?}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-wait-group? \var{obj})}
\returns \scheme{#t} if \var{obj} is an async wait group, \scheme{#f} otherwise
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-wait-group-add!}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-wait-group-add! \var{group} \var{delta})}
\formdef{async-wait-group-done!}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-wait-group-done! \var{group})}
\returns unspecified
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{async-wait-group-add!} changes the counter by the exact integer
\var{delta}; a negative result is an error.  \scheme{async-wait-group-done!}
subtracts one.  Counter updates are thread-safe.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-wait-group-wait-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-wait-group-wait-operation \var{group})}
\formdef{async-wait-group-wait}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-wait-group-wait \var{group})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

The operation becomes ready when the counter is zero.  The direct procedure
performs that operation and returns an unspecified value.  A positive addition
that begins a generation precedes its corresponding wait.  A group may be
reused after every wait from the preceding generation has returned.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{spawn-task/async-wait-group}{\categoryprocedure}{(spawn-task/async-wait-group \var{group} \var{thunk} \var{option} \var{value} \dots)}
\returns a new task
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

The procedure adds one to \var{group} before spawning the task, then calls
\scheme{async-wait-group-done!} when the child leaves \var{thunk} by normal
return, exception, or cancellation.  It accepts the options of
\scheme{spawn-task}.  Adding before publication ensures that an immediate wait
cannot observe a transient zero counter.  A termination action installed before
publication also handles cancellation before the child's first turn.

\bigskip\noindent\textbf{One-time initialization.}\par

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{make-async-once}{\categoryprocedure}{(make-async-once)}
\returns a new async once object
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-once?}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-once? \var{obj})}
\returns \scheme{#t} if \var{obj} is an async once object, \scheme{#f} otherwise
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-once-run!}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-once-run! \var{once} \var{thunk})}
\returns unspecified
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

Exactly one caller invokes \var{thunk}; concurrent callers suspend until that
invocation finishes.  The once object becomes done after either a normal
return or an exception, including cancellation.  The executing caller receives
the exception, while other and later callers return normally without retrying
\var{thunk}.  Values from \var{thunk} are discarded.  Recursive use by the
executing task is an error.

\bigskip\noindent\textbf{Condition variables.}\par

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{make-async-condition}{\categoryprocedure}{(make-async-condition \var{mutex})}
\returns a new async condition variable
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\var{mutex} must be an async mutex or an async read/write mutex.  A read/write
mutex is used in write mode.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-condition?}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-condition? \var{obj})}
\returns \scheme{#t} if \var{obj} is an async condition variable, \scheme{#f} otherwise
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-condition-mutex}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-condition-mutex \var{condition})}
\returns the mutex associated with \var{condition}
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-condition-wait}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-condition-wait \var{condition})}
\returns unspecified
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

The current task must own the associated mutex.  Waiting registers the task,
releases the mutex, suspends, and reacquires the mutex before returning.
Cancellation also reacquires the mutex before raising its condition.  Since a
notification carries no state, callers test their shared predicate in a loop.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-condition-signal!}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-condition-signal! \var{condition})}
\formdef{async-condition-broadcast!}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-condition-broadcast! \var{condition})}
\returns unspecified
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

The signal procedure wakes the first live FIFO waiter.  The broadcast
procedure wakes all registered waiters.  Both procedures are thread-safe and
do not require ownership of the associated mutex.

The following example combines a wait group, one-time initialization, and a
condition variable.  The predicate loop remains necessary even when every
state update is protected by the mutex.

\schemedisplay
(run-async
  (lambda ()
    (let* ([mutex (make-async-mutex)]
           [ready (make-async-condition mutex)]
           [done (make-async-wait-group 2)]
           [once (make-async-once)]
           [value #f])
      (do ([i 0 (+ i 1)]) ((= i 2))
        (spawn-task
          (lambda ()
            (async-once-run! once
              (lambda ()
                (call-with-async-mutex mutex
                  (lambda ()
                    (set! value 42)
                    (async-condition-broadcast! ready)))))
            (async-wait-group-done! done))))
      (call-with-async-mutex mutex
        (lambda ()
          (let loop ()
            (unless value
              (async-condition-wait ready)
              (loop)))))
      (async-wait-group-wait done)
      value))) ;=> 42
\endschemedisplay


\section{Asynchronous Syntax\label{SECTASYNCSYNTAX}}

\index{asynchronous syntax}%
\index{tasks!syntactic forms}%
\index{channels!iteration}%
The \scheme{(chezscheme async syntax)} library provides hygienic,
expression-oriented forms over the task, context, operation, channel, and sync
libraries.  The forms preserve all values produced by their bodies and do not
intercept exceptions or cancellation conditions.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async}{\categorysyntax}{(async \var{body_1} \var{body_2} \dots)}
\formdef{async/options}{\categorysyntax}{(async/options ([\var{option} \var{expr}] \dots) \var{body_1} \var{body_2} \dots)}
\returns the values produced by the body
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{async} evaluates the body in a thunk passed to \scheme{run-async}.
\scheme{async/options} additionally accepts the \scheme{clock},
\scheme{parallelism}, and \scheme{preemption-ticks} options.  Option
expressions are evaluated exactly once in source order before the scheduler
starts.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{go}{\categorysyntax}{(go \var{body_1} \var{body_2} \dots)}
\formdef{go/options}{\categorysyntax}{(go/options ([\var{option} \var{expr}] \dots) \var{body_1} \var{body_2} \dots)}
\formdef{await}{\categorysyntax}{(await \var{task-expr})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{go} spawns a migratable child task and returns it.
\scheme{go/options} accepts the \scheme{name}, \scheme{group},
\scheme{context}, and \scheme{migratable?} options of \scheme{spawn-task},
defaulting \scheme{migratable?} to \scheme{#t}.  Its option expressions are
evaluated exactly once in source order.  \scheme{await} joins the task
produced by \var{task-expr} and returns all of the task's values.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{select-operation}{\categorysyntax}{(select-operation \var{clause} \dots)}
\formdef{select}{\categorysyntax}{(select \var{clause} \dots)}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

The clause grammar is:

\schemedisplay
[(on operation-expr variable ...) body ...]
[(recv channel-expr value-variable open?-variable) body ...]
[(send channel-expr value-expr) body ...]
[(after seconds-expr) body ...]
[else body ...]
\endschemedisplay

\scheme{select-operation} constructs an operation and \scheme{select}
immediately performs it.  An \scheme{on} arm accepts any operation and binds
the values it produces.  The \scheme{recv}, \scheme{send}, and \scheme{after}
arms expand to channel receive, channel put, and sleep operations.  An
\scheme{else} arm must be last and is immediately ready.  Without
\scheme{else}, selection suspends until an arm is ready.

Arm input expressions are evaluated exactly once in clause order.  Only the
selected body runs, and losing operations are nacked.  The selected body's
values become the result of the constructed operation, so the result of
\scheme{select-operation} can be wrapped, combined with other operations, or
performed under a cancellation context.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{with-timeout}{\categorysyntax}{(with-timeout \var{seconds-expr} \var{body_1} \var{body_2} \dots)}
\formdef{with-async-context}{\categorysyntax}{(with-async-context \var{context-expr} \var{body_1} \var{body_2} \dots)}
\formdef{with-cancel-scope}{\categorysyntax}{(with-cancel-scope (\var{cancel!}) \var{body_1} \var{body_2} \dots)}
\returns the values produced by the body
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{with-timeout} installs a timeout context for the body, and
\scheme{with-async-context} installs the context produced by
\var{context-expr}.  \scheme{with-cancel-scope} installs a fresh child context
and lexically binds \var{cancel!} to a procedure that accepts zero or one
cancellation-reason argument.  The scope context is canceled with
\scheme{scope-exited} when control leaves the body, bounding descendants
created within the scope.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{channel-for}{\categorysyntax}{(channel-for (\var{value} \var{channel-expr}) \var{body_1} \var{body_2} \dots)}
\returns unspecified
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{channel-for} evaluates \var{channel-expr} once, binds each received
value to \var{value}, and evaluates the body until the channel is closed and
empty.  Buffered values are drained before iteration terminates.  The form
does not close the channel or cancel its producer.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{with-async-mutex}{\categorysyntax}{(with-async-mutex \var{mutex-expr} \var{body_1} \var{body_2} \dots)}
\formdef{with-async-rw-mutex}{\categorysyntax}{(with-async-rw-mutex \var{mutex-expr} \var{body_1} \var{body_2} \dots)}
\formdef{with-async-read-mutex}{\categorysyntax}{(with-async-read-mutex \var{mutex-expr} \var{body_1} \var{body_2} \dots)}
\returns the values produced by the body
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

The \var{mutex-expr} expression is evaluated once.  The body runs through
the corresponding scoped synchronization procedure.  The first two forms use
exclusive mode, while \scheme{with-async-read-mutex} uses read mode.  The mode
is released after a normal return or an exception, including cancellation,
and remains owned across asynchronous suspension and timed preemption.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{with-async-wait-group}{\categorysyntax}{(with-async-wait-group \var{group-expr} \var{body_1} \var{body_2} \dots)}
\returns a new task
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

The form evaluates \var{group-expr} once, adds one in the calling task, and
spawns a migratable child for its body.  The child calls
\scheme{async-wait-group-done!} whenever it terminates.  The addition is
visible before the task is published, so the caller may wait immediately.

The following examples provide a quick reference for the syntax library.
The task forms accept the same option names as their procedural counterparts
while making migratable child tasks the default:

\schemedisplay
(import (chezscheme)
        (chezscheme async)
        (chezscheme async channels)
        (chezscheme async context)
        (chezscheme async operations)
        (chezscheme async sync)
        (chezscheme async syntax))

(async/options
  (;; run-async options: clock, parallelism, and preemption-ticks
   [parallelism 2]
   [preemption-ticks 10000])
  (let ([worker
         (go/options
           (;; spawn-task options: name, group, context, and migratable?
            [name 'worker]
            [migratable? #t])
           (values 'answer 42))])
    ;; await preserves every value returned by the task.
    (call-with-values (lambda () (await worker)) list))) ;=> (answer 42)
\endschemedisplay

\scheme{with-async-mutex} protects state shared by migratable tasks without
blocking a scheduler thread.  The body may suspend while retaining ownership:

\schemedisplay
(async/options ([parallelism 2])
  (let ([mutex (make-async-mutex)] [total 0])
    (let ([workers
           (map
             (lambda (amount)
               (go
                 (with-async-mutex mutex
                   ;; The lock remains owned while this task yields.
                   (task-yield)
                   (set! total (+ total amount)))))
             '(10 20 12))])
      (for-each (lambda (worker) (await worker)) workers)
      total))) ;=> 42
\endschemedisplay

Read and write scopes compose with the same task forms.  A queued writer takes
priority over readers that have not already acquired read mode:

\schemedisplay
(async/options ([parallelism 2])
  (let ([rw (make-async-rw-mutex)] [items '()])
    (let ([writer
           (go
             (with-async-rw-mutex rw
               ;; Exclusive access protects the update.
               (set! items (cons 'ready items))))])
      (await writer)
      (with-async-read-mutex rw
        ;; Multiple readers may inspect the protected state concurrently.
        items)))) ;=> (ready)
\endschemedisplay

The wait-group form publishes membership before spawning its child, so the
caller can wait without first yielding to the scheduler:

\schemedisplay
(async
  (let ([done (make-async-wait-group)] [value #f])
    (with-async-wait-group done
      ;; done! runs when this child returns, raises, or is canceled.
      (set! value 42))
    (async-wait-group-wait done)
    value)) ;=> 42
\endschemedisplay

\scheme{select} supports general operations as well as channel and timer
shorthands.  An \scheme{else} arm turns the form into a nonblocking poll;
omitting it waits until another arm is ready:

\schemedisplay
(async
  (let ([input (make-channel)]
        [output (make-channel 1)]
        [worker (go (async-sleep 0.01) 'worker-done)])
    (select
      ;; Bind every successful value produced by an arbitrary operation.
      [(on (task-join-operation worker) result)
       (list 'joined result)]

      ;; Receive returns the value and whether the channel remains open.
      [(recv input value open?)
       (if open? (list 'received value) 'input-closed)]

      ;; The branch runs after the value has been accepted or buffered.
      [(send output 'message)
       'sent]

      ;; Remove the else arm to make this a real timeout race.
      [(after 1.0)
       'timed-out]

      ;; This arm is ready immediately, so the select never blocks.
      [else 'not-ready])))
\endschemedisplay

\scheme{select-operation} leaves selection as a first-class operation.  It
can be wrapped, combined with another choice, or performed under a context:

\schemedisplay
(async/options ([clock 'virtual])
  (let* ([input (make-channel 1)]
         [_ (channel-put input 21)]
         [selected
          (select-operation
            [(recv input value open?)
             (if open? value 0)]
            [(after 1) 0])]
         [doubled
          (wrap-operation selected (lambda (value) (* value 2)))])
    (perform-operation doubled))) ;=> 42
\endschemedisplay

The scope forms preserve body values.  Cancellation raised by a timeout or
by the procedure bound by \scheme{with-cancel-scope} remains an ordinary
asynchronous cancellation condition:

\schemedisplay
(async/options ([clock 'virtual])
  (guard (c
           [(async-cancellation-condition? c)
            (async-cancellation-reason c)])
    (with-timeout 10
      (let ([context (make-async-context)])
        (with-async-context context
          (with-cancel-scope (cancel!)
            ;; Descendants inherit the scope's cancellation context.
            (go (async-sleep 100))
            (cancel! 'shutdown)
            ;; The next blocking operation observes cancellation.
            (async-sleep 1))))))) ;=> shutdown
\endschemedisplay

\scheme{channel-for} implements channel range iteration.  It drains buffered
values before recognizing the closed-and-empty state; closing remains the
producer's responsibility:

\schemedisplay
(async
  (let ([values (make-channel 2)])
    (go
      (channel-put values 1)
      (channel-put values 2)
      ;; channel-for stops only after these buffered values are consumed.
      (channel-close! values))
    (let ([items '()])
      (channel-for (value values)
        (set! items (cons value items)))
      (reverse items)))) ;=> (1 2)
\endschemedisplay


\section{Asynchronous I/O Subsystem\label{SECTASYNCIO}}

\index{libuv}%
\index{I/O!asynchronous}%
The asynchronous I/O libraries integrate {\ChezScheme} with an internal libuv
event loop hosted by each scheduler.  When an I/O operation cannot complete
immediately, the initiating task is suspended and resumed automatically when
the corresponding I/O event is triggered by the operating system.

For every direct blocking I/O procedure, a corresponding \scheme{-operation}
constructor exists that returns a composable operation accepting identical
arguments and producing equivalent results unless otherwise specified.

Explicit closing of handles and streams via their respective close procedures
is the primary mechanism for managing system resources.  In addition,
{\ChezScheme}'s storage management guardians and scheduler shutdown routines
automatically reclaim abandoned native handles.

Native handles are bound to the scheduler group in which they are created.
A task executing on any scheduler within that group may interact with a handle;
the runtime automatically routes the request to the handle's owning event loop.
Handles cannot be shared across different scheduler groups.  Closing a port
created by an asynchronous port adapter automatically closes the underlying
native handle.


\section{I/O Conditions and Errors\label{SECTASYNCIOERRORS}}

\index{I/O conditions}%
\index{conditions!I/O}%
The \scheme{(chezscheme async io errors)} library defines condition types and
diagnostics for asynchronous I/O errors.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{make-async-io-condition}{\categoryprocedure}{(make-async-io-condition \var{operation} \var{handle} \var{path} \var{code})}
\formdef{async-io-condition?}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-io-condition? \var{obj})}
\formdef{async-io-condition-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-io-condition-operation \var{condition})}
\formdef{async-io-condition-handle}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-io-condition-handle \var{condition})}
\formdef{async-io-condition-path}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-io-condition-path \var{condition})}
\formdef{async-io-condition-code}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-io-condition-code \var{condition})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{make-async-io-condition} constructs an asynchronous I/O condition.
\scheme{async-io-condition?} returns \scheme{#t} if \var{obj} is an
asynchronous I/O condition and \scheme{#f} otherwise.
The accessors extract the operation name, the associated native handle (or
\scheme{#f}), the target filesystem path (or \scheme{#f}), and the native
libuv integer error code or logical failure symbol (such as \scheme{closed} or
\scheme{busy}).

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-io-error-name}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-io-error-name \var{code})}
\formdef{async-io-error-message}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-io-error-message \var{code})}
\returns a string
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

These procedures accept a native libuv integer error code \var{code} and
return libuv's standard symbolic error name (such as \scheme{"ENOENT"}) and
explanatory descriptive message, respectively.


\section{Streams and Listeners\label{SECTASYNCSTREAMS}}

\index{TCP streams}%
\index{pipe streams}%
\index{listeners}%
The \scheme{(chezscheme async io stream)} library provides nonblocking TCP
network streams, local-domain (Unix domain socket or named pipe) streams, and
port adapters.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{tcp-listen}{\categoryprocedure}{(tcp-listen \var{host} \var{port})}
\formdef{tcp-listen}{\categoryprocedure}{(tcp-listen \var{host} \var{port} \var{backlog})}
\formdef{pipe-listen}{\categoryprocedure}{(pipe-listen \var{path})}
\formdef{pipe-listen}{\categoryprocedure}{(pipe-listen \var{path} \var{backlog})}
\returns a listener object
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{tcp-listen} creates a TCP listener bound to \var{host} (an IPv4 or
IPv6 address string) and \var{port} (an exact integer from 0 to 65535).
\scheme{pipe-listen} creates a local-domain stream listener bound to the
filesystem path \var{path}.  The optional \var{backlog} parameter specifies the
connection queue size (defaulting to 128).

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{tcp-listener?}{\categoryprocedure}{(tcp-listener? \var{obj})}
\formdef{tcp-listener-close}{\categoryprocedure}{(tcp-listener-close \var{listener})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{tcp-listener?} returns \scheme{#t} if \var{obj} is a TCP or local pipe
listener and \scheme{#f} otherwise.
\scheme{tcp-listener-close} closes \var{listener}, waking any blocked
accept operations, and returns an unspecified value.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{tcp-accept-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(tcp-accept-operation \var{listener})}
\formdef{tcp-connect-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(tcp-connect-operation \var{host} \var{port})}
\formdef{pipe-connect-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(pipe-connect-operation \var{path})}
\formdef{tcp-accept}{\categoryprocedure}{(tcp-accept \var{listener})}
\formdef{tcp-connect}{\categoryprocedure}{(tcp-connect \var{host} \var{port})}
\formdef{pipe-connect}{\categoryprocedure}{(pipe-connect \var{path})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{tcp-accept-operation} and \scheme{tcp-accept} await and accept an
incoming connection on \var{listener}, returning an asynchronous stream.
\scheme{tcp-connect-operation} and \scheme{tcp-connect} establish a TCP
connection to \var{host} on \var{port}.
\scheme{pipe-connect-operation} and \scheme{pipe-connect} establish a
connection to a local-domain pipe at \var{path}.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-stream?}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-stream? \var{obj})}
\formdef{tcp-stream?}{\categoryprocedure}{(tcp-stream? \var{obj})}
\formdef{pipe-stream?}{\categoryprocedure}{(pipe-stream? \var{obj})}
\formdef{stream-closed?}{\categoryprocedure}{(stream-closed? \var{stream})}
\returns \scheme{#t} if \var{obj} is a matching stream, \scheme{#f} otherwise
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

These predicates test for asynchronous streams, TCP streams, local pipe
streams, and closed streams, respectively.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{stream-read-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(stream-read-operation \var{stream})}
\formdef{stream-write-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(stream-write-operation \var{stream} \var{bytevector})}
\formdef{stream-read}{\categoryprocedure}{(stream-read \var{stream})}
\formdef{stream-write}{\categoryprocedure}{(stream-write \var{stream} \var{bytevector})}
\formdef{stream-shutdown}{\categoryprocedure}{(stream-shutdown \var{stream})}
\formdef{stream-close}{\categoryprocedure}{(stream-close \var{stream})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{stream-read-operation} and \scheme{stream-read} read the next available
chunk of data from \var{stream}, returning a non-empty bytevector, or the
end-of-file object if the remote peer has closed its end.
\scheme{stream-write-operation} returns an operation that produces the number
of bytes written.  \scheme{stream-write} transmits the contents of
\var{bytevector} directly and returns an unspecified value.
\scheme{stream-shutdown} closes the outbound writing channel after all pending
writes have completed.
\scheme{stream-close} immediately closes both read and write channels of
\var{stream} and frees the underlying handle.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-stream->binary-input-port}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-stream->binary-input-port \var{stream})}
\formdef{async-stream->binary-output-port}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-stream->binary-output-port \var{stream})}
\formdef{async-stream->binary-input/output-port}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-stream->binary-input/output-port \var{stream})}
\returns a binary port
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

These procedures construct standard {\ChezScheme} binary ports backed by
\var{stream}.  Calling an adapter transfers ownership of \var{stream} to the
new port; subsequent direct calls to stream procedures on \var{stream} are
disallowed.  Closing the returned port automatically closes \var{stream}.
Stream ports do not support position or seeking operations.

The following example implements an asynchronous TCP ping-pong exchange:

\schemedisplay
(import (chezscheme)
        (chezscheme async)
        (chezscheme async io stream))

(run-async
  (lambda ()
    (let* ([listener (tcp-listen "127.0.0.1" 9000)]
           [server
            (spawn-task
              (lambda ()
                (let ([stream (tcp-accept listener)])
                  (let ([request (utf8->string (stream-read stream))])
                    (stream-write stream (string->utf8 "pong"))
                    (stream-close stream)
                    request))))]
           [client (tcp-connect "127.0.0.1" 9000)])
      (stream-write client (string->utf8 "ping"))
      (let ([reply (utf8->string (stream-read client))])
        (stream-close client)
        (tcp-listener-close listener)
        (list (task-join server) reply))))) ;=> ("ping" "pong")
\endschemedisplay


\section{Domain Name Resolution\label{SECTASYNCDNS}}

\index{DNS}%
\index{name resolution}%
The \scheme{(chezscheme async io dns)} library provides nonblocking forward and
reverse DNS resolution.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{dns-lookup-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(dns-lookup-operation \var{node})}
\formdef{dns-lookup-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(dns-lookup-operation \var{node} \var{service})}
\formdef{dns-lookup}{\categoryprocedure}{(dns-lookup \var{node})}
\formdef{dns-lookup}{\categoryprocedure}{(dns-lookup \var{node} \var{service})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\var{node} is a host name string, and \var{service} is an optional port number
string or service name (or \scheme{#f}).
\scheme{dns-lookup} resolves \var{node} and returns a list of address entries
of the form \scheme{(\var{host} \var{port} \var{family})}, where \var{host} is
a numeric IP string, \var{port} is an exact integer, and \var{family} is
\scheme{4} for IPv4 or \scheme{6} for IPv6.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{dns-reverse-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(dns-reverse-operation \var{host} \var{port})}
\formdef{dns-reverse-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(dns-reverse-operation \var{host} \var{port} \var{flags})}
\formdef{dns-reverse}{\categoryprocedure}{(dns-reverse \var{host} \var{port})}
\formdef{dns-reverse}{\categoryprocedure}{(dns-reverse \var{host} \var{port} \var{flags})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\var{host} is an IP address string, \var{port} is an integer, and \var{flags}
is an optional list containing any of \scheme{name-required},
\scheme{numeric-host}, or \scheme{numeric-service}.
\scheme{dns-reverse} performs a reverse DNS lookup and returns two values: the
resolved host name and service name as strings.


\section{UDP Sockets\label{SECTASYNCUDP}}

\index{UDP}%
\index{sockets!UDP}%
\index{datagrams}%
The \scheme{(chezscheme async io udp)} library provides nonblocking UDP socket
operations, datagram I/O, and multicast management.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{udp-open}{\categoryprocedure}{(udp-open)}
\formdef{udp-open}{\categoryprocedure}{(udp-open \var{host} \var{port})}
\formdef{udp-open}{\categoryprocedure}{(udp-open \var{host} \var{port} \var{bind-flags})}
\returns a UDP socket
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{udp-open} creates an asynchronous UDP socket.  When \var{host} and
\var{port} are supplied, the socket is immediately bound to the specified
local address.  \var{bind-flags} is an optional list that may contain
\scheme{ipv6-only} or \scheme{reuse-address}.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{udp-socket?}{\categoryprocedure}{(udp-socket? \var{obj})}
\formdef{udp-close}{\categoryprocedure}{(udp-close \var{socket})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{udp-socket?} returns \scheme{#t} if \var{obj} is a UDP socket and
\scheme{#f} otherwise.
\scheme{udp-close} closes \var{socket}, waking any blocked receive
operations, and returns an unspecified value.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{udp-bind!}{\categoryprocedure}{(udp-bind! \var{socket} \var{host} \var{port})}
\formdef{udp-bind!}{\categoryprocedure}{(udp-bind! \var{socket} \var{host} \var{port} \var{bind-flags})}
\formdef{udp-connect!}{\categoryprocedure}{(udp-connect! \var{socket} \var{host} \var{port})}
\formdef{udp-disconnect!}{\categoryprocedure}{(udp-disconnect! \var{socket})}
\returns unspecified
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{udp-bind!} binds \var{socket} to a local \var{host} and \var{port}.
\scheme{udp-connect!} establishes a default remote peer address for
\var{socket}, enabling connection-oriented two-argument send operations.
\scheme{udp-disconnect!} clears the default remote peer.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{udp-send-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(udp-send-operation \var{socket} \var{bytevector})}
\formdef{udp-send-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(udp-send-operation \var{socket} \var{bytevector} \var{host} \var{port})}
\formdef{udp-send}{\categoryprocedure}{(udp-send \var{socket} \var{bytevector})}
\formdef{udp-send}{\categoryprocedure}{(udp-send \var{socket} \var{bytevector} \var{host} \var{port})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{udp-send} transmits \var{bytevector} over \var{socket}.  The
two-argument forms require a connected socket.  The four-argument forms send
the datagram to the explicitly specified remote \var{host} and \var{port}.
The direct procedures return the number of bytes sent as an exact integer.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{udp-receive-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(udp-receive-operation \var{socket})}
\formdef{udp-receive}{\categoryprocedure}{(udp-receive \var{socket})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{udp-receive} receives a datagram from \var{socket}, returning four
values: the received payload as a \var{bytevector}, the sender's IP address
string \var{host}, the sender's \var{port} as an exact integer, and the
address \var{family} (\scheme{4} for IPv4 or \scheme{6} for IPv6).

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{udp-local-address}{\categoryprocedure}{(udp-local-address \var{socket})}
\formdef{udp-peer-address}{\categoryprocedure}{(udp-peer-address \var{socket})}
\returns three values: \var{host}, \var{port}, and \var{family}
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

These procedures return the local bound address and the connected peer
address of \var{socket}, respectively, as three values: \var{host},
\var{port}, and \var{family}.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{udp-membership-set!}{\categoryprocedure}{(udp-membership-set! \var{socket} \var{multicast} \var{interface} \var{source} \var{action})}
\formdef{udp-multicast-interface-set!}{\categoryprocedure}{(udp-multicast-interface-set! \var{socket} \var{interface})}
\formdef{udp-option-set!}{\categoryprocedure}{(udp-option-set! \var{socket} \var{option} \var{value})}
\returns unspecified
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{udp-membership-set!} joins or leaves a multicast group.  \var{action}
must be \scheme{join} or \scheme{leave}.  \var{source} is an empty string for
standard multicast, or a specific source address for source-specific
multicast.
\scheme{udp-multicast-interface-set!} sets the default outbound multicast
interface.
\scheme{udp-option-set!} configures socket options; \var{option} must be one
of \scheme{multicast-loop}, \scheme{multicast-ttl}, \scheme{broadcast}, or
\scheme{ttl}.


\section{Files and Directories\label{SECTASYNCFS}}

\index{files!asynchronous}%
\index{directories!asynchronous}%
\index{filesystem operations}%
The \scheme{(chezscheme async io fs)} library provides nonblocking file and
directory I/O, metadata queries, filesystem manipulation, and temporary file
management.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-file?}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-file? \var{obj})}
\formdef{file-open-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-open-operation \var{path} \var{flags})}
\formdef{file-open-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-open-operation \var{path} \var{flags} \var{mode})}
\formdef{file-open}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-open \var{path} \var{flags})}
\formdef{file-open}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-open \var{path} \var{flags} \var{mode})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{async-file?} returns \scheme{#t} if \var{obj} is an asynchronous file
handle and \scheme{#f} otherwise.
\scheme{file-open} opens the file at \var{path}, returning an asynchronous
file handle.  \var{flags} must be a list containing any of \scheme{read},
\scheme{write}, \scheme{create}, \scheme{truncate}, \scheme{append}, or
\scheme{exclusive}.  The optional \var{mode} specifies file permissions when
creating a new file (defaults to \scheme{#o666}).

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{file-read-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-read-operation \var{file} \var{length})}
\formdef{file-read}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-read \var{file} \var{length})}
\formdef{file-write-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-write-operation \var{file} \var{bytevector})}
\formdef{file-write}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-write \var{file} \var{bytevector})}
\formdef{file-close-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-close-operation \var{file})}
\formdef{file-close}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-close \var{file})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{file-read} reads up to \var{length} bytes from \var{file}, advancing
the file position.  It returns a bytevector, or the end-of-file object if the
end of file is reached.  \var{length} must be a positive integer.
\scheme{file-write} writes \var{bytevector} to \var{file} and returns the
number of bytes written as an exact integer.  Operations that manipulate a
file's implicit offset are automatically serialized.
\scheme{file-close} closes \var{file}, releases the underlying handle, and
returns an unspecified value.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-file->binary-input-port}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-file->binary-input-port \var{file})}
\formdef{async-file->binary-output-port}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-file->binary-output-port \var{file})}
\formdef{async-file->binary-input/output-port}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-file->binary-input/output-port \var{file})}
\returns a binary port
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

These adapters construct binary ports backed by \var{file}, transferring
ownership of \var{file} to the port.  Files opened without \scheme{append}
support standard port positioning operations; append-mode files do not.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{file-stat-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-stat-operation \var{path-or-file})}
\formdef{file-stat}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-stat \var{path-or-file})}
\formdef{file-lstat-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-lstat-operation \var{path})}
\formdef{file-lstat}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-lstat \var{path})}
\formdef{file-system-stat-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-system-stat-operation \var{path})}
\formdef{file-system-stat}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-system-stat \var{path})}
\returns an alist
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{file-stat} retrieves metadata for \var{path-or-file} (following
symbolic links).
\scheme{file-lstat} queries the link itself rather than the target.
Both procedures return an alist containing the fields \scheme{dev},
\scheme{mode}, \scheme{nlink}, \scheme{uid}, \scheme{gid}, \scheme{rdev},
\scheme{ino}, \scheme{size}, \scheme{blksize}, \scheme{blocks}, \scheme{atime},
\scheme{mtime}, and \scheme{ctime}.  Timestamps are represented as
\scheme{(\var{seconds} . \var{nanoseconds})} pairs.
\scheme{file-system-stat} queries filesystem metrics for \var{path}, returning
an alist with \scheme{type}, \scheme{block-size}, \scheme{blocks},
\scheme{blocks-free}, \scheme{blocks-available}, \scheme{files},
\scheme{files-free}, and \scheme{fragment-size}.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{file-delete-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-delete-operation \var{path})}
\formdef{file-delete}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-delete \var{path})}
\formdef{file-rename-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-rename-operation \var{old-path} \var{new-path})}
\formdef{file-rename}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-rename \var{old-path} \var{new-path})}
\formdef{file-copy-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-copy-operation \var{source} \var{target})}
\formdef{file-copy-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-copy-operation \var{source} \var{target} \var{flags})}
\formdef{file-copy}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-copy \var{source} \var{target})}
\formdef{file-copy}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-copy \var{source} \var{target} \var{flags})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{file-delete} removes the file at \var{path}.
\scheme{file-rename} atomically moves or renames \var{old-path} to \var{new-path}.
\scheme{file-copy} copies \var{source} to \var{target}.  \var{flags} is an
optional list containing any of \scheme{exclusive}, \scheme{clone}, or
\scheme{clone-force}.  The direct procedures return an unspecified value.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{file-sync-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-sync-operation \var{file})}
\formdef{file-sync}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-sync \var{file})}
\formdef{file-data-sync-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-data-sync-operation \var{file})}
\formdef{file-data-sync}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-data-sync \var{file})}
\formdef{file-truncate-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-truncate-operation \var{file} \var{length})}
\formdef{file-truncate}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-truncate \var{file} \var{length})}
\formdef{file-send-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-send-operation \var{output} \var{input} \var{offset} \var{length})}
\formdef{file-send}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-send \var{output} \var{input} \var{offset} \var{length})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{file-sync} flushes all file metadata and data to disk.
\scheme{file-data-sync} flushes only file data to disk.
\scheme{file-truncate} truncates \var{file} to \var{length} bytes.
\scheme{file-send} transfers up to \var{length} bytes from \var{input} starting
at \var{offset} to \var{output} using zero-copy OS mechanisms where available,
returning the number of bytes transferred.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{file-access-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-access-operation \var{path} \var{modes})}
\formdef{file-access?}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-access? \var{path} \var{modes})}
\formdef{file-mode-set-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-mode-set-operation \var{path-or-file} \var{mode})}
\formdef{file-mode-set!}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-mode-set! \var{path-or-file} \var{mode})}
\formdef{file-times-set-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-times-set-operation \var{path-or-file} \var{atime} \var{mtime})}
\formdef{file-times-set-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-times-set-operation \var{path-or-file} \var{atime} \var{mtime} \var{follow?})}
\formdef{file-times-set!}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-times-set! \var{path-or-file} \var{atime} \var{mtime})}
\formdef{file-times-set!}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-times-set! \var{path-or-file} \var{atime} \var{mtime} \var{follow?})}
\formdef{file-owner-set-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-owner-set-operation \var{path-or-file} \var{uid} \var{gid})}
\formdef{file-owner-set-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-owner-set-operation \var{path-or-file} \var{uid} \var{gid} \var{follow?})}
\formdef{file-owner-set!}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-owner-set! \var{path-or-file} \var{uid} \var{gid})}
\formdef{file-owner-set!}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-owner-set! \var{path-or-file} \var{uid} \var{gid} \var{follow?})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{file-access?} checks permissions for \var{path}; \var{modes} is a list
containing \scheme{exists}, \scheme{read}, \scheme{write}, or \scheme{execute}.
It returns \scheme{#t} if permissions are granted and \scheme{#f} otherwise.
\scheme{file-mode-set!} changes file permissions to the integer \var{mode}.
\scheme{file-times-set!} sets access and modification times (expressed in
seconds as real numbers).  \var{follow?} defaults to \scheme{#t}.
\scheme{file-owner-set!} sets the numeric user ID \var{uid} and group ID \var{gid}.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{file-link-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-link-operation \var{source} \var{target})}
\formdef{file-link}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-link \var{source} \var{target})}
\formdef{file-symbolic-link-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-symbolic-link-operation \var{source} \var{target})}
\formdef{file-symbolic-link-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-symbolic-link-operation \var{source} \var{target} \var{flags})}
\formdef{file-symbolic-link}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-symbolic-link \var{source} \var{target})}
\formdef{file-symbolic-link}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-symbolic-link \var{source} \var{target} \var{flags})}
\formdef{file-read-link-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-read-link-operation \var{path})}
\formdef{file-read-link}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-read-link \var{path})}
\formdef{file-real-path-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-real-path-operation \var{path})}
\formdef{file-real-path}{\categoryprocedure}{(file-real-path \var{path})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{file-link} creates a hard link from \var{source} to \var{target}.
\scheme{file-symbolic-link} creates a symbolic link; \var{flags} may contain
\scheme{directory} or \scheme{junction} on supported platforms.
\scheme{file-read-link} returns the destination path stored in a symbolic link as a string.
\scheme{file-real-path} canonicalizes \var{path}, resolving all symbolic links
and relative segments to return an absolute path string.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{directory-create-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(directory-create-operation \var{path})}
\formdef{directory-create-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(directory-create-operation \var{path} \var{mode})}
\formdef{directory-create}{\categoryprocedure}{(directory-create \var{path})}
\formdef{directory-create}{\categoryprocedure}{(directory-create \var{path} \var{mode})}
\formdef{directory-delete-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(directory-delete-operation \var{path})}
\formdef{directory-delete}{\categoryprocedure}{(directory-delete \var{path})}
\formdef{directory-scan-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(directory-scan-operation \var{path})}
\formdef{directory-scan}{\categoryprocedure}{(directory-scan \var{path})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{directory-create} creates a directory at \var{path} with permissions
\var{mode} (defaulting to \scheme{#o755}).
\scheme{directory-delete} removes the empty directory at \var{path}.
\scheme{directory-scan} reads all entries in the directory at \var{path},
returning a list of \scheme{(\var{name} . \var{type})} pairs.  \var{type} is
one of the symbols \scheme{file}, \scheme{directory}, \scheme{link},
\scheme{fifo}, \scheme{socket}, \scheme{character-device},
\scheme{block-device}, or \scheme{unknown}.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-directory?}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-directory? \var{obj})}
\formdef{directory-open-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(directory-open-operation \var{path})}
\formdef{directory-open}{\categoryprocedure}{(directory-open \var{path})}
\formdef{directory-read-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(directory-read-operation \var{directory})}
\formdef{directory-read-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(directory-read-operation \var{directory} \var{count})}
\formdef{directory-read}{\categoryprocedure}{(directory-read \var{directory})}
\formdef{directory-read}{\categoryprocedure}{(directory-read \var{directory} \var{count})}
\formdef{directory-close-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(directory-close-operation \var{directory})}
\formdef{directory-close}{\categoryprocedure}{(directory-close \var{directory})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

These procedures support streaming directory iteration.
\scheme{async-directory?} returns \scheme{#t} if \var{obj} is an asynchronous
directory stream and \scheme{#f} otherwise.
\scheme{directory-open} opens a directory stream.
\scheme{directory-read} reads up to \var{count} entries (defaulting to 64),
returning an empty list when all entries have been read.
\scheme{directory-close} closes the directory stream.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{temporary-directory-create-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(temporary-directory-create-operation \var{pattern})}
\formdef{temporary-directory-create}{\categoryprocedure}{(temporary-directory-create \var{pattern})}
\formdef{temporary-file-open-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(temporary-file-open-operation \var{pattern})}
\formdef{temporary-file-open}{\categoryprocedure}{(temporary-file-open \var{pattern})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\var{pattern} must be a template path string ending in six \scheme{X}
characters (for example, \scheme{"/tmp/chez-XXXXXX"}).
\scheme{temporary-directory-create} creates a uniquely named temporary
directory and returns its path string.
\scheme{temporary-file-open} creates and opens a temporary file exclusively,
returning the open asynchronous file handle and its path string.

The following example creates a temporary file, accesses it through a
seekable binary port, and cleans up the file:

\schemedisplay
(import (chezscheme)
        (chezscheme async)
        (chezscheme async io fs))

(run-async
  (lambda ()
    (let-values ([(file path)
                  (temporary-file-open "/tmp/chez-example-XXXXXX")])
      (let ([port (async-file->binary-input/output-port file)])
        (put-bytevector port (string->utf8 "hello"))
        (flush-output-port port)
        (set-port-position! port 0)
        (let ([text (utf8->string (get-bytevector-n port 5))])
          (close-port port)
          (file-delete path)
          text))))) ;=> "hello"
\endschemedisplay


\section{File-Descriptor Polling\label{SECTASYNCPOLL}}

\index{polling}%
\index{file descriptor polling}%
The \scheme{(chezscheme async io poll)} library allows asynchronous monitoring
of native file descriptors (such as third-party sockets or device handles)
without taking ownership of the descriptor.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{fd-poll-open}{\categoryprocedure}{(fd-poll-open \var{fd})}
\formdef{fd-poll-handle?}{\categoryprocedure}{(fd-poll-handle? \var{obj})}
\formdef{fd-poll-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(fd-poll-operation \var{poll-handle} \var{events})}
\formdef{fd-poll}{\categoryprocedure}{(fd-poll \var{poll-handle} \var{events})}
\formdef{fd-poll-close}{\categoryprocedure}{(fd-poll-close \var{poll-handle})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\var{fd} is a native integer file descriptor or socket handle.
\scheme{fd-poll-open} creates a poll handle.  A poll handle permits one active
waiter at a time and does not close \var{fd} when released.
\scheme{fd-poll-handle?} returns \scheme{#t} if \var{obj} is a poll handle and
\scheme{#f} otherwise.
\scheme{fd-poll} monitors \var{poll-handle} for the events in \var{events},
which is a list containing any of \scheme{readable}, \scheme{writable},
\scheme{disconnect}, or \scheme{prioritized}.  It returns the subset of events
that have become active.
\scheme{fd-poll-close} frees the poll handle and returns an unspecified value.


\section{Processes\label{SECTASYNCPROCESS}}

\index{subprocesses}%
\index{process management}%
The \scheme{(chezscheme async io process)} library manages nonblocking child
subprocesses and their standard I/O streams.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{process-spawn}{\categoryprocedure}{(process-spawn \var{file} \var{arguments})}
\formdef{process-spawn}{\categoryprocedure}{(process-spawn \var{file} \var{arguments} \var{options})}
\returns four values: \var{process}, \var{stdin}, \var{stdout}, and \var{stderr}
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\var{file} is an executable path string, and \var{arguments} is a list of
argument strings.  The optional \var{options} parameter is an alist supporting
the following keys:

\begin{description}
\item[\scheme{cwd}] specifies the child working directory as a string.
\item[\scheme{environment}] is an alist of string key/value environment pairs,
  or \scheme{#f} to inherit the parent process's environment.
\item[\scheme{flags}] is a list of process flags: \scheme{detached},
  \scheme{windows-hide}, or \scheme{windows-verbatim-arguments}.
\item[\scheme{stdin}, \scheme{stdout}, \scheme{stderr}] configure standard
  streams and must be \scheme{pipe} (the default), \scheme{ignore},
  \scheme{inherit}, or an integer file descriptor.
\end{description}

\scheme{process-spawn} returns four values: the process handle \var{process},
and three stream objects \var{stdin}, \var{stdout}, and \var{stderr} (or
\scheme{#f} for non-pipe descriptors).

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-process?}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-process? \var{obj})}
\formdef{process-id}{\categoryprocedure}{(process-id \var{process})}
\formdef{process-wait-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(process-wait-operation \var{process})}
\formdef{process-wait}{\categoryprocedure}{(process-wait \var{process})}
\formdef{process-kill}{\categoryprocedure}{(process-kill \var{process} \var{signal})}
\formdef{process-id-kill}{\categoryprocedure}{(process-id-kill \var{pid} \var{signal})}
\formdef{process-close}{\categoryprocedure}{(process-close \var{process})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{async-process?} returns \scheme{#t} if \var{obj} is an asynchronous process
handle and \scheme{#f} otherwise.
\scheme{process-id} returns the operating-system PID of \var{process} as an exact integer.
\scheme{process-wait} suspends until the child process terminates, returning
its integer \var{exit-status} and \var{termination-signal}.
\scheme{process-kill} and \scheme{process-id-kill} send the integer signal
\var{signal} to \var{process} or \var{pid}.
\scheme{process-close} closes the process handle; it does not terminate the
child process.

\schemedisplay
(import (chezscheme)
        (chezscheme async)
        (chezscheme async io process)
        (chezscheme async io stream))

(run-async
  (lambda ()
    (let-values ([(child stdin stdout stderr)
                  (process-spawn "/bin/sh" '("-c" "printf hello"))])
      (stream-close stdin)
      (let ([text (utf8->string (stream-read stdout))])
        (let-values ([(status signal) (process-wait child)])
          (stream-close stdout)
          (stream-close stderr)
          (process-close child)
          (list text status signal)))))) ;=> ("hello" 0 0)
\endschemedisplay


\section{Signals\label{SECTASYNCSIGNAL}}

\index{signals}%
\index{signal handling}%
The \scheme{(chezscheme async io signal)} library provides nonblocking operating-system
signal watchers.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{signal-open}{\categoryprocedure}{(signal-open \var{signum})}
\formdef{signal-watcher?}{\categoryprocedure}{(signal-watcher? \var{obj})}
\formdef{signal-receive-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(signal-receive-operation \var{watcher})}
\formdef{signal-receive}{\categoryprocedure}{(signal-receive \var{watcher})}
\formdef{signal-close}{\categoryprocedure}{(signal-close \var{watcher})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{signal-open} creates a watcher for the integer signal number
\var{signum}.
\scheme{signal-watcher?} returns \scheme{#t} if \var{obj} is a signal watcher
and \scheme{#f} otherwise.
\scheme{signal-receive} performs a one-shot wait for the next occurrence of
the signal, returning the received signal number as an exact integer.
\scheme{signal-close} deactivates and frees the signal watcher, returning an
unspecified value.


\section{Filesystem Watching\label{SECTASYNCWATCH}}

\index{filesystem watching}%
\index{file events}%
The \scheme{(chezscheme async io watch)} library monitors filesystem trees for
file modifications, creations, renames, and deletions using native OS event
notifications or periodic polling.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{fs-event-open}{\categoryprocedure}{(fs-event-open \var{path})}
\formdef{fs-event-open}{\categoryprocedure}{(fs-event-open \var{path} \var{flags})}
\formdef{fs-event-watcher?}{\categoryprocedure}{(fs-event-watcher? \var{obj})}
\formdef{fs-event-receive-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(fs-event-receive-operation \var{watcher})}
\formdef{fs-event-receive}{\categoryprocedure}{(fs-event-receive \var{watcher})}
\formdef{fs-event-close}{\categoryprocedure}{(fs-event-close \var{watcher})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{fs-event-open} creates a watcher using native OS notifications for
\var{path}.  \var{flags} may contain \scheme{watch-entry}, \scheme{stat}, or
\scheme{recursive}.
\scheme{fs-event-watcher?} returns \scheme{#t} if \var{obj} is an event watcher
and \scheme{#f} otherwise.
\scheme{fs-event-receive} waits for the next event, returning the affected
filename \var{name} and a list \var{events} containing \scheme{rename} or
\scheme{change}.
\scheme{fs-event-close} closes the watcher and returns an unspecified value.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{fs-poll-open}{\categoryprocedure}{(fs-poll-open \var{path} \var{interval-ms})}
\formdef{fs-poll-watcher?}{\categoryprocedure}{(fs-poll-watcher? \var{obj})}
\formdef{fs-poll-receive-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(fs-poll-receive-operation \var{watcher})}
\formdef{fs-poll-receive}{\categoryprocedure}{(fs-poll-receive \var{watcher})}
\formdef{fs-poll-close}{\categoryprocedure}{(fs-poll-close \var{watcher})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{fs-poll-open} creates a stat-based polling watcher checking \var{path}
every \var{interval-ms} milliseconds.
\scheme{fs-poll-watcher?} returns \scheme{#t} if \var{obj} is a poll watcher
and \scheme{#f} otherwise.
\scheme{fs-poll-receive} waits for an observed change, returning the previous
and current file-stat alists (Section~\ref{SECTASYNCFS}).
\scheme{fs-poll-close} closes the watcher and returns an unspecified value.


\section{Terminal I/O\label{SECTASYNCTTY}}

\index{terminal I/O}%
\index{TTY}%
The \scheme{(chezscheme async io tty)} library provides terminal stream
manipulation, raw mode control, and window size querying.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{tty-open}{\categoryprocedure}{(tty-open \var{fd})}
\formdef{tty-stream?}{\categoryprocedure}{(tty-stream? \var{obj})}
\formdef{tty-mode-set!}{\categoryprocedure}{(tty-mode-set! \var{tty} \var{mode})}
\formdef{tty-window-size}{\categoryprocedure}{(tty-window-size \var{tty})}
\formdef{tty-virtual-terminal-state}{\categoryprocedure}{(tty-virtual-terminal-state)}
\formdef{tty-virtual-terminal-state-set!}{\categoryprocedure}{(tty-virtual-terminal-state-set! \var{state})}
\formdef{tty-reset-mode!}{\categoryprocedure}{(tty-reset-mode!)}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{tty-open} opens a TTY stream on the integer file descriptor \var{fd}.
A TTY stream satisfies \scheme{async-stream?} and can be used with all stream
I/O and port adapter procedures.
\scheme{tty-stream?} returns \scheme{#t} if \var{obj} is a TTY stream and
\scheme{#f} otherwise.
\scheme{tty-mode-set!} configures the terminal mode; \var{mode} must be
\scheme{normal}, \scheme{raw}, or \scheme{io}.
\scheme{tty-window-size} returns the terminal dimensions in characters as two
exact integers: \var{width} and \var{height}.
\scheme{tty-virtual-terminal-state} queries whether virtual terminal (ANSI
escape) processing is supported, returning \scheme{supported} or \scheme{unsupported}.
\scheme{tty-virtual-terminal-state-set!} updates this state.
\scheme{tty-reset-mode!} restores the terminal modes saved at process startup.


\section{System Information and Random Data\label{SECTASYNCSYSTEM}}

\index{system information}%
\index{random bytevectors}%
\index{loop metrics}%
The \scheme{(chezscheme async io system)} library provides cryptographically
secure random byte generation, high-resolution timers, hardware and operating
system information, and event loop performance metrics.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{random-bytevector-operation}{\categoryprocedure}{(random-bytevector-operation \var{length})}
\formdef{random-bytevector}{\categoryprocedure}{(random-bytevector \var{length})}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\var{length} must be an exact nonnegative integer.
\scheme{random-bytevector} returns a bytevector of length \var{length} filled
with cryptographically secure random bytes generated by the operating system.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{system-high-resolution-time}{\categoryprocedure}{(system-high-resolution-time)}
\formdef{system-uptime}{\categoryprocedure}{(system-uptime)}
\formdef{system-load-average}{\categoryprocedure}{(system-load-average)}
\returns see below
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{system-high-resolution-time} returns the current value of a monotonic
clock in nanoseconds as an exact nonnegative integer.
\scheme{system-uptime} returns the system uptime in seconds as a real number.
\scheme{system-load-average} returns the 1-, 5-, and 15-minute system load
averages as a list of three real numbers.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{system-memory-info}{\categoryprocedure}{(system-memory-info)}
\formdef{system-process-info}{\categoryprocedure}{(system-process-info)}
\formdef{system-path-info}{\categoryprocedure}{(system-path-info)}
\formdef{system-uname}{\categoryprocedure}{(system-uname)}
\formdef{system-cpu-info}{\categoryprocedure}{(system-cpu-info)}
\formdef{system-interface-info}{\categoryprocedure}{(system-interface-info)}
\formdef{system-resource-usage}{\categoryprocedure}{(system-resource-usage)}
\returns an alist or list of alists
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

These procedures query operating system and hardware diagnostics:

\scheme{system-memory-info} returns an alist of memory statistics with byte counts
for \scheme{total}, \scheme{free}, \scheme{constrained}, \scheme{available}, and
\scheme{resident-set}.

\scheme{system-process-info} returns an alist of process information with
\scheme{pid}, \scheme{parent-pid}, and \scheme{available-parallelism}.

\scheme{system-path-info} returns an alist of system paths containing
\scheme{executable}, \scheme{current-directory}, \scheme{home-directory},
\scheme{temporary-directory}, and \scheme{hostname}.

\scheme{system-uname} returns an alist with \scheme{system}, \scheme{release},
\scheme{version}, and \scheme{machine}.

\scheme{system-cpu-info} returns a list of alists, one per CPU core, with
entries for \scheme{model}, \scheme{speed}, \scheme{user}, \scheme{nice},
\scheme{system}, \scheme{idle}, and \scheme{irq}.

\scheme{system-interface-info} returns a list of alists for each network
interface, containing \scheme{name}, \scheme{address}, \scheme{netmask},
\scheme{family}, \scheme{internal?}, and \scheme{physical-address}.

\scheme{system-resource-usage} returns an alist of process resource metrics.
The entries \scheme{user-time} and \scheme{system-time} are pairs of
\scheme{(\var{seconds} . \var{microseconds})}.  The remaining entries are
\scheme{maximum-resident-set-size}, \scheme{minor-page-faults},
\scheme{major-page-faults}, \scheme{swaps}, \scheme{input-blocks},
\scheme{output-blocks}, \scheme{signals}, \scheme{voluntary-context-switches},
and \scheme{involuntary-context-switches}.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\entryheader
\formdef{async-loop-metrics}{\categoryprocedure}{(async-loop-metrics)}
\returns an alist of loop metrics
\listlibraries
\endentryheader

\scheme{async-loop-metrics} must be called from within an active asynchronous
scheduler.  It returns an alist of performance metrics for the calling
scheduler's libuv loop: \scheme{now} (loop timestamp in milliseconds),
\scheme{idle-time} (idle time in nanoseconds), \scheme{backend-timeout}
(in milliseconds), \scheme{backend-fd}, \scheme{alive?}, \scheme{loop-count},
\scheme{events}, and \scheme{events-waiting}.

\schemedisplay
(import (chezscheme)
        (chezscheme async)
        (chezscheme async io system))

(run-async
  (lambda ()
    (let ([token (random-bytevector 32)]
          [metrics (async-loop-metrics)])
      (list (bytevector-length token)
            (cdr (assq 'loop-count metrics)))))) ;=> (32 loop-count)
\endschemedisplay
